In order to establish the tort of interference with the contract, the Plaintiff- Appellant must prove the following elements:
1. A business relationship;
2. The wrongdoers knowledge of the relationship;
3. The wrongdoer's intentional improper action taken to prevent a contract formation, procure a contractual breach or terminate a business relationship;
4. Lack of privilege; and,
5. Damages.
Spafford v. Cuyahoga Comm. College (Cuyahoga App. 2005), 2005-Ohio-1672 at 1 49.
The tort of intentional interference occurs when the wrongdoer (1) prevents the Plaintiff-Appellant from performing his own contract; or (2) or prevents the Plaintiff- Appellant from entering into a prospective contract. Id.
Based on the elements listed above, the Plaintiff-Appellant fails to make such a claim. Plaintiff-Appellant alleges that Young interfered with his potential lease with the Lakes of Aurora, however, as has been shown above, Young did not intentionally take any improper actions to prevent that lease and Young was privileged for any action she did take. Young merely filled out the form that the Lakes of Aurora sent to her unsolicited and she filled it out truthfully. Plaintiff-Appellant has not produced any evidence of an intentional improper action by Young with respect to Plaintiff-Appellant's attempt to rent an apartment at the Lakes of Aurora. By truthfully stating facts and her opinion, Young
